M="cvs-physio-2"
SUB="Physiology"
T={
"apv":[("The plateau (phase 2) of the ventricular action potential is maintained mainly by:",["Ca²⁺ influx through L-type channels balanced by K⁺ efflux","Na⁺ influx through fast channels balanced by Cl⁻ influx","K⁺ influx through inward rectifiers balanced by Na⁺ efflux","Ca²⁺ release from the SR balanced by Na⁺ influx"],0,"L-type Ca²⁺ inward current is offset by delayed-rectifier K⁺ outward current."),
 ("Phase 0 of the ventricular myocyte action potential is due to:",["Opening of voltage-gated fast Na⁺ channels","Opening of voltage-gated L-type Ca²⁺ channels","Opening of HCN (funny current) channels","Closing of inward-rectifier K⁺ channels"],0,"Fast Na⁺ current produces the rapid upstroke in atrial, ventricular and Purkinje cells."),
 ("Unlike skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle cannot be tetanized by rapid repetitive stimulation. The main reason is that:",["Its effective refractory period lasts almost as long as contraction","Its thin filaments lack troponin C, so Ca²⁺ cannot bind","It depends on gap junctions to spread excitation between cells","Its resting membrane potential is much less negative than skeletal"],0,"The long plateau extends refractoriness through most of the twitch.",2)],
"app":[("Spontaneous phase 4 depolarization of SA nodal cells is mainly due to:",["The funny current (I_f) through HCN channels","Fast voltage-gated Na⁺ channel opening","Delayed-rectifier K⁺ channel opening","Inward-rectifier K⁺ channel opening"],0,"I_f is a mixed Na⁺/K⁺ inward current activated by hyperpolarization."),
 ("The upstroke of the SA nodal action potential is slow because:",["Fast Na⁺ channels are inactivated at the nodal resting potential","Nodal cells lack any voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels in the membrane","Nodal cells have no functioning K⁺ channels during phase 3","The HCN channels close abruptly at the start of phase 0"],0,"At −55 to −60 mV most fast Na⁺ channels are inactivated, so phase 0 relies on Ca²⁺ current."),
 ("A 30-year-old woman with paroxysmal SVT receives IV adenosine and her heart rate falls abruptly. In SA and AV nodal cells, adenosine acts by:",["Increasing K⁺ conductance and lowering the phase 4 slope","Blocking fast Na⁺ channels and slowing phase 0","Increasing cAMP and opening more HCN channels","Prolonging the plateau through more Ca²⁺ entry"],0,"Like ACh, adenosine activates K⁺ channels → hyperpolarization and slower diastolic depolarization.",2)],
"ecg":[("The PR interval mainly reflects conduction delay in the:",["AV node","Bundle of His","Purkinje fibers","Ventricular muscle"],0,"Normal PR 120–200 ms; most of it is AV nodal delay."),
 ("Prominent U waves on the ECG are most associated with:",["Hypokalemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypercalcemia","Hypermagnesemia"],0,"“Hyp-U-kalemia”; also seen with bradycardia."),
 ("The AV node of a 59-year-old man with an inferior STEMI and new second-degree AV block is most likely supplied by the:",["Posterior descending artery","Left anterior descending artery","Left circumflex marginal branch","Right marginal artery"],0,"The AV node blood supply is via the PDA (from the RCA in right-dominant hearts).",2),
 ("Rank conduction velocity from fastest to slowest:",["His–Purkinje > atria > ventricles > AV node","Atria > His–Purkinje > AV node > ventricles","Ventricles > His–Purkinje > atria > AV node","AV node > atria > ventricles > His–Purkinje"],0,"“He Parks At Ventura AVenue.”")],
"refr":[("During the effective refractory period, a second stimulus fails to produce a propagated action potential because:",["Most fast Na⁺ channels are still inactivated","The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase has stopped pumping","Gap junctions are closed during the plateau","The membrane is hyperpolarized below E_K"],0,"Na⁺ channels must recover (repolarize) before they can reopen."),
 ("A stimulus delivered in the relative refractory period of a ventricular cell produces an action potential that is:",["Smaller, with a slower upstroke and conduction","Larger, with a faster upstroke and conduction","Identical to a normal action potential","Prolonged, with an exaggerated plateau"],0,"Only part of the Na⁺ channels have recovered."),
 ("A premature impulse reaches tissue where one pathway is still refractory and the other has recovered, setting up a reentrant circuit. A drug that lengthens the effective refractory period helps terminate it because:",["The returning impulse meets tissue that is still refractory","The impulse is conducted faster around the circuit","The resting membrane potential becomes more negative","Automaticity of the SA node increases"],0,"Prolonging ERP removes the excitable gap so the circulating wave dies out.",3)],
"ecgread":[("On a standard ECG at 25 mm/s, one small (1 mm) box represents:",["0.04 s","0.20 s","0.10 s","0.02 s"],0,"Large box (5 mm) = 0.20 s."),
 ("The QRS is positive in lead I and negative in aVF. The axis is most likely:",["Left axis deviation","Right axis deviation","Normal axis","Extreme axis"],0,"Positive I and negative aVF place the axis between 0° and −90° (LAD if beyond −30°)."),
 ("A 25-year-old man with long-standing pulmonic stenosis has a tall R in V1. His QRS is negative in lead I and positive in aVF. This axis pattern most likely reflects:",["Right ventricular hypertrophy","Left ventricular hypertrophy","Left anterior fascicular block","Normal conduction in a young adult"],0,"RVH shifts the mean QRS vector rightward (I negative, aVF positive).",2)],
"natri":[("ANP exerts its effects through which second messenger?",["cGMP","cAMP","IP₃","Ca²⁺–calmodulin"],0,"Natriuretic peptide receptors are guanylyl cyclases."),
 ("ANP increases GFR by:",["Dilating afferent and constricting efferent arterioles","Constricting afferent and dilating efferent arterioles","Increasing renin and angiotensin II release","Increasing plasma oncotic pressure in the glomerulus"],0,"Both changes raise glomerular capillary pressure."),
 ("A 67-year-old man presents with dyspnea. A low plasma BNP level in this setting is most useful because it:",["Makes heart failure an unlikely cause of the dyspnea","Confirms heart failure with preserved ejection fraction","Indicates that atrial pressure is markedly elevated","Predicts a good response to loop diuretics"],0,"BNP has a very high negative predictive value for HF.",2)],
"baro":[("Afferent signals from the carotid sinus baroreceptors travel to the medulla in the:",["Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Vagus nerve (CN X)","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","Accessory nerve (CN XI)"],0,"Carotid sinus → Hering nerve → CN IX → NTS; aortic arch → CN X."),
 ("Central chemoreceptors respond primarily to changes in:",["PCO₂ and pH of brain interstitial fluid","PO₂ of arterial blood perfusing the brain","Mean arterial blood pressure in the carotids","Plasma K⁺ concentration in arterial blood"],0,"CO₂ crosses the BBB; H⁺ does not; PO₂ does not stimulate central chemoreceptors."),
 ("An 80-year-old man faints while shaving. ECG during carotid massage shows sinus pauses. The mechanism of his syncope is:",["Increased baroreceptor firing causing vagal bradycardia","Decreased baroreceptor firing causing sympathetic surge","Peripheral chemoreceptor stimulation by hypoxemia","Atrial stretch reflex causing tachycardia"],0,"Carotid sinus hypersensitivity: pressure on the sinus mimics hypertension.",2),
 ("A man with a large epidural hematoma has BP 210/110 mm Hg, HR 44/min and irregular breathing. The bradycardia is caused by:",["Baroreceptor response to the central pressor reflex","Direct compression of the SA nodal artery","Peripheral chemoreceptor stimulation by hypoxemia","Hypokalemia from increased sympathetic tone"],0,"Cushing reflex: cerebral ischemia → sympathetic HTN → baroreflex bradycardia.",3)],
"press":[("Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure overestimates left ventricular end-diastolic pressure in:",["Mitral stenosis","Aortic stenosis","Tricuspid regurgitation","Pulmonic stenosis"],0,"The stenotic mitral valve creates a gradient between LA (≈PCWP) and LV."),
 ("Normal right ventricular pressure is closest to:",["25/5 mm Hg","120/10 mm Hg","25/12 mm Hg","40/20 mm Hg"],0,"RV systolic ~25, diastolic near 0–5."),
 ("A patient in shock has a pulmonary artery catheter placed. When the balloon is inflated, the tracing changes from 42/26 to a damped waveform with a mean of 24 mm Hg. Assuming no valve disease, this value best estimates:",["Left atrial pressure","Right atrial pressure","Pulmonary artery systolic pressure","Right ventricular end-diastolic pressure"],0,"PCWP reflects LA pressure (normal 4–12); 24 mm Hg suggests a cardiogenic cause.",2)],
"longbp":[("In the long term, mean arterial pressure is set mainly by:",["The renal output curve for salt and water","The baroreceptor set point","The total peripheral resistance alone","The heart rate set by the SA node"],0,"Pressure natriuresis returns MAP to the equilibrium point where output = intake."),
 ("Arterial baroreceptors are poor regulators of long-term blood pressure because they:",["Reset to a sustained new pressure within days","Respond only to pressures below 60 mm Hg","Cannot alter heart rate or vascular tone","Fire only during exercise"],0,"Baroreceptor adaptation limits their long-term role."),
 ("A man with primary hyperaldosteronism cannot suppress aldosterone when he eats more salt. Compared with a healthy person, a high-salt diet in this patient raises arterial pressure:",["Much more, because his renal output curve is flatter","Much less, because his renal output curve is steeper","Equally, because baroreceptors buffer both","Not at all, because ANP fully compensates"],0,"Without RAAS suppression, the renal function curve is shifted and flattened → salt-sensitive BP.",3)],
"autoreg":[("Hypoxia causes vasoconstriction in the:",["Pulmonary circulation","Coronary circulation","Cerebral circulation","Skeletal muscle circulation"],0,"Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction diverts blood to ventilated alveoli."),
 ("Cerebral blood flow is regulated mostly by:",["Arterial PCO₂ (pH)","Sympathetic nerve activity","Circulating angiotensin II","Plasma K⁺ concentration"],0,"Hypercapnia dilates; hyperventilation constricts cerebral vessels."),
 ("A man with raised intracranial pressure after head trauma is briefly hyperventilated. Intracranial pressure falls because:",["Lower PCO₂ constricts cerebral arterioles","Lower PO₂ constricts cerebral arterioles","Higher PCO₂ dilates cerebral veins","Higher pH dilates cerebral arterioles"],0,"Hypocapnia → cerebral vasoconstriction → less cerebral blood volume.",2),
 ("During heavy exercise, blood flow to skeletal muscle rises mainly because of:",["Local metabolites (CO₂, H⁺, adenosine, lactate, K⁺)","Increased sympathetic vasoconstrictor tone to muscle","Increased circulating angiotensin II and vasopressin","A myogenic response to raised arterial pressure"],0,"CHALK metabolites override sympathetic tone during exercise.")],
"starlingf":[("Which change promotes net fluid movement out of capillaries?",["Increased interstitial oncotic pressure","Increased plasma oncotic pressure","Increased interstitial hydrostatic pressure","Decreased capillary hydrostatic pressure"],0,"πi pulls fluid out; πc and Pi oppose filtration."),
 ("The reflection coefficient (σ) in the Starling equation describes:",["How impermeable the capillary wall is to protein","How permeable the capillary wall is to water","The surface area available for filtration","The hydrostatic gradient across the wall"],0,"σ = 1 means proteins cannot cross; σ near 0 means they cross freely."),
 ("A 48-year-old woman has periorbital and leg edema, serum albumin 1.8 g/dL and urine protein 6 g/day. Her edema is primarily due to:",["Decreased plasma oncotic pressure","Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure","Increased capillary permeability","Lymphatic obstruction"],0,"Nephrotic syndrome → hypoalbuminemia → lower πc.",2)],
"edema":[("Edema after radical mastectomy with axillary node dissection is due mainly to:",["Increased interstitial oncotic pressure","Decreased plasma oncotic pressure","Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure","Decreased capillary permeability"],0,"Lymphatic removal lets protein accumulate in the interstitium."),
 ("Which factor helps protect tissues against edema when capillary pressure rises?",["Increased lymph flow","Increased plasma protein leakage","Decreased reflection coefficient","Increased capillary permeability"],0,"Lymph flow, low interstitial compliance and protein washdown form safety factors."),
 ("A man with severe burns over 40% of his body develops widespread edema despite normal heart function. The main cause is:",["Increased capillary permeability","Decreased capillary permeability","Increased plasma oncotic pressure","Decreased capillary hydrostatic pressure"],0,"Burns raise Kf and let protein escape.",2)],
"coronary":[("Blood flow in the left coronary artery is greatest during:",["Early diastole","Isovolumetric contraction","Rapid ejection","Late systole"],0,"Systolic compression of intramyocardial vessels limits LV flow."),
 ("The region of the left ventricle most vulnerable to ischemia is the:",["Subendocardium","Subepicardium","Right atrial wall","Right ventricular free wall"],0,"It is most compressed in systole and farthest from epicardial vessels."),
 ("A 56-year-old man with coronary stenosis has angina only when his heart rate exceeds 120/min. Tachycardia worsens his ischemia mainly because it:",["Shortens diastole, when most LV perfusion occurs","Lengthens systole, when most LV perfusion occurs","Reduces myocardial O₂ demand per minute","Increases diastolic aortic pressure"],0,"Higher HR raises demand and cuts diastolic perfusion time.",2)],
}
